If writing to a buffer causes the internal capacity of the buffer to increase, read buffer contents (even if a reader index is marked) can be discarded. Here's a simple test:

```
@Test
public void testReadBytesNotDiscardedOnInternalBufferCapacityIncrease() {
    byte[] bytes = new byte[CAPACITY];
    random.nextBytes(bytes);
    buffer.writeBytes(bytes);

    // ensure first byte is not zero
    if (bytes[0] == 0) {
        bytes[0] = 1;
    }

    buffer.markReaderIndex();
    buffer.readByte();
    buffer.writeByte(1); // increases capacity
    buffer.resetReaderIndex();

    int readable = buffer.readableBytes();
    assertEquals(CAPACITY + 1, readable);

    byte[] expectedBytes = new byte[readable];
    System.arraycopy(bytes, 0, expectedBytes, 0, CAPACITY);
    expectedBytes[CAPACITY] = 1;

    byte[] actualBytes = new byte[readable];
    buffer.readBytes(actualBytes);
    assertArrayEquals(expectedBytes, actualBytes);
}
```

I made a patch that mostly fixes it by simply copying the entire buffer contents (regardless of the reader and writer indexes) on capacity increase, but the SlicedByteBufTest is failing, and I'm not sure what the contract is with a sliced ByteBuf.
